{
  "compute-money": {
    "state": {
      "date": "2026-11-23",
      "text": [
        "As of 23 November the listing has happened and the pricing question is answered on the tape. Anthropic priced 980,000,000 shares of Class A common stock at $83.00 on 18 November, above the $70.00 to $78.00 range, and closed the offering on 20 November at an aggregate 1,127,000,000 shares for approximately $93.5 billion gross, of which approximately $91.6 billion was received by Anthropic, alongside approximately $26.0 billion from a concurrent private placement of 313,253,007 Class N shares to AMD, Broadcom, Google, Micron, Microsoft, NVIDIA and TSMC Partners. The shares opened at $111.50 on 19 November and closed at $97.17, then closed the Friday at $88.91.",
        "The other half of the week was the sell-off. The S&P 500 fell 3.7% on the Friday into a correction and the Nasdaq composite slid 10% over five sessions in its worst week since 2020, led by Nvidia's 23% weekly plunge, while SoftBank Group closed 14.48% lower. Nvidia reported $115.8 billion of quarterly revenue, up 103%, guided to $130.0 billion with no China data centre compute revenue assumed, and its board said it would not reinstate Jensen Huang; Reuters reported SEC enforcement staff scrutinising potential accounting issues, and CNBC counted a seventh straight weekly decline, the longest such stretch since 2008.",
        "The commitments behind the capital are disclosed and unmet. Anthropic's filings show $70.7 billion of compute and infrastructure commitments due within 12 months and $451.6 billion before the end of 2030, against total infrastructure commitments of $1.05 trillion to $1.08 trillion, with Amazon and Google together holding 28% after the IPO. Oracle said it is fully funded for its $300bn OpenAI cloud contract after capping new raises at $20bn, and SoftBank said a roughly $1tn SK hynix buyout is no longer under active consideration."
      ],
      "changed": "The IPO question this arc has carried since September is settled: Anthropic priced above range at $83.00 and closed at approximately $93.5 billion gross, the largest offering on record. What is new since the 16 November snapshot is that the listing and the first correction in the AI trade fell in the same five sessions, and that Nvidia's slide now carries two investigations alongside it."
